/** @mainpage %Qore krb5 Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section krb5intro krb5 Binary Module Introduction

    The \c krb5 module provides Kerberos 5 and GSSAPI primitives for secure
    enterprise authentication integrations.

    The Qore-language utility module is named \c Krb5Util rather than \c Krb5
    so that it cannot conflict with the binary \c krb5 module on
    case-insensitive filesystems.

    This initial release focuses on:
    - Kerberos default realm and credential-cache discovery
    - Kerberos initial credential acquisition from passwords and keytabs
    - Kerberos credential metadata and cache store/list operations
    - Kerberos default keytab discovery and service-key entry management
    - in-memory credential-cache initialization for tests and service setup
    - filesystem sandbox enforcement for file-backed credential caches
    - filesystem sandbox enforcement for file-backed keytabs
    - Kerberos principal parsing, enterprise principal parsing, and validation
    - explicit GSSAPI credential import from Kerberos caches and client keytabs
    - GSSAPI target-name handling
    - initiator-side GSSAPI token stepping with configurable flags and mechanisms
    - GSSAPI channel bindings for hardened SASL/GSSAPI deployments
    - GSSAPI message wrap/unwrap helpers for negotiated integrity and confidentiality layers

    @section krb5securitymodel Security Model

    The module is intended for security-sensitive use. It is designed to be
    used with strict TLS, Kerberos, and Active Directory integrations without
    weakening enterprise security policies. Initial credential acquisition
    supports noninteractive service use, KDC canonicalization, PAC requests,
    enctype restrictions, and FAST armor credential caches. GSSAPI initiator
    contexts can be bound to explicit credentials to avoid global default-cache
    assumptions in service processes, can include channel-binding application
    data, and completed contexts can protect messages when SASL/GSSAPI integrity
    or confidentiality layers are required.

